{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"One scale, for everyone who has lived in this world, with the rarity levels counted over the whole span; 90 Events judged. The top level is kept to eight:
- the dispersal;
- the Holocene;
- farming;
- the first states;
- the joining of the hemispheres;
- the industrial revolution;
- the mortality transition;
- the Second World War.
Each is a change most later outcomes depend on, as the cause readings show. The Black Death, the slave trade, printing and the twentieth-century transitions sit at the second level. Each judgment is exploring: another audience (economic historians, say, or those who follow one region) would rank differently and can have its own scale. Setting the top level drew the model's question about why two of them came about, which this round answered."}

